7571"} "pentathlon"] ". He was not entered in the long jump."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The favorite was America’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/78579"} "DeHart Hubbard"] ", having won the AAU in 1922-24 and the 1924 US Olympic Trials, but he stood only third after qualifying. Norway’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/74149"} "Sverre Hansen"] " led the qualifying with 7.26 (23-10), with " [:a {:href "/athletes/78465"} "Edward “Ned” Gourdin"] ", 1921 AAU Champion, in second place. In the final rounds, Gourdin improved first to 7.275 (23-10½), and then Hubbard won with his 7.445 (24-5¼) jump. In 1925, Hubbard broke the world record with 7.89 (25-10¾)."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The sixth-place finisher, " [:a {:href "/athletes/69271"} "Chris Mackintosh"] " was an accomplished all-round sportsman. He won the Public Schools lawn tennis doubles in 1921, represented Oxford University at athletics, rugby football, and skiing, played rugby for Scotland on the wing in 1924, and was a member of the British crew that won the 4-man bobsleigh at the 1937 World Championships. Two of his daughters, " [:a {:href "/athletes/81733"} "Sheena"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/81734"} "Vora"] ", were Olympic skiers (1948/52 and 1952, respectively)."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Qualifying"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/78579"} "DeHart Hubbard"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "7.120 (3)"] [:td {} "7.445 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/78465"} "Ned Gourdin"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-f